+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by how much they matter. The first four are exploits or
+data loss and should be fixed before anything is built on this script. Line
+numbers refer to the file as it is at commit `bedeaad`.
+
+This review comes from reading the code. stylua, selene, and luau-lsp were not
+run (see the end of the file).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Client arguments are trusted, and a NaN amount gives unlimited blocks
+
+**Where:** `buyRemote` handler (lines 84-103), `placeRemote` handler
+(105-125), `removeRemote` handler (127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:** The handlers annotate client arguments as `bt: string`,
+`amt: number`, `pos: Vector3`, `part: Instance`, but a client can send anything.
+The annotations make the type checker trust values it should be checking.
+
+- **NaN amount (the serious one).** `amt < 1` and `amt > MAX_PURCHASE` are both
+  false for NaN, so NaN passes the range check. `price` becomes NaN, and
+  `coins < price` is false, so the purchase goes through. `setCoins` saves
+  `coins - NaN`, and `playerStock[bt]` becomes NaN. In the place handler
+  `(NaN) < 1` is false, so that player can now place unlimited blocks. Their
+  saved coin value is also broken: either the DataStore call fails, or it
+  stores a value that passes every later `coins < price` check.
+- **Fractional amounts.** `amt = 1.5` passes, so coins and stock end up as
+  fractions.
+- **Unknown block type.** `PRICES[bt]` is `nil` for any string that isn't a
+  block, so `PRICES[bt] * amt` errors. That only crashes the handler, but any
+  client can trigger it whenever it wants.
+- **Bad position.** A non-Vector3 `pos` errors in `PlacementRules.snap`. A
+  Vector3 with NaN parts passes every check in `PlacementRules.check`, because
+  every comparison with NaN is false, and puts a block at a NaN position.
+- **Bad part.** `part` can be `nil` or not an Instance, and then `part.Parent`
+  errors.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type every argument after the player as `unknown` and
+narrow it before use, returning early when a check fails. Shape the checks so
+they reject NaN:
+
+```lua
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- also rejects NaN
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	...
+```
+
+`BlockTypes.isBlockType` already exists and isn't used yet. For `pos`, check
+`typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and that each part is finite (`x == x` and
+`math.abs(x) ~= math.huge`). For `part`, check `typeof(part) == "Instance"`
+before reading `part.Parent`. Adding `--!strict` (finding 9) makes the checker
+require these checks once the arguments are `unknown`.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Coins are read and written straight to the DataStore on every purchase
+
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42-48), used at lines 90 and 94.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Double spending.** `GetAsync` yields. If two `BuyBlocks` requests arrive
+  together, both read the same balance, both pass the check, and both write
+  `coins - price`. The player pays once and gets both batches. A client can do
+  this on purpose by firing the remote twice in one frame.
+- **No error handling.** `GetAsync` and `SetAsync` throw when the DataStore is
+  down or throttled. Nothing catches the error, so the handler dies partway
+  through.
+- **Rate limits.** Every purchase costs two DataStore requests. A client
+  spamming the remote uses up the server's request budget, and saves for every
+  other player on that server start to queue or fail.
+- **Player leaves mid-request.** If the player leaves during `GetAsync`,
+  `stock[plr]` is `nil` by line 96. That errors after `SetAsync` has already
+  taken the coins.
+- **No save on shutdown.** Nothing saves in `BindToClose`. That doesn't matter
+  today, but it will once finding 3 is fixed.
+
+**What to do instead:** Load the player's data once in `PlayerAdded`, keep it
+in a server-side table, and spend from that table synchronously, so the check
+and the deduction have no yield between them. Save in `PlayerRemoving`, in
+`game:BindToClose`, and on a periodic autosave. Use `UpdateAsync` inside
+`pcall`, with a comment on which errors you expect, and retry on failure. A
+session-locking library such as ProfileStore handles all of this and also stops
+two servers from writing the same player's data. Name the functions that yield
+`loadCoinsAsync` / `saveCoinsAsync`.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Bought blocks are lost when the player leaves
+
+**Where:** `stock` (line 40), `PlayerRemoving` (lines 146-148).
+
+**What's wrong:** Coins are saved, but stock only lives in memory and is
+cleared when the player leaves. Anyone who buys blocks and leaves (or loses
+connection, or is on a server that shuts down) before placing them loses both
+the coins and the blocks.
+
+**What to do instead:** Save stock in the same record as coins, under one key,
+so that one save covers both and they can't drift apart. Fix this together
+with finding 2. The other option is to refund unplaced stock on leave, but
+saving it is simpler and matches what players expect.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Removing someone else's abandoned block gives you a free block, and the "owner left" path errors
+
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50-57), `removeRemote` handler (lines
+127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Free blocks.** Line 139 refunds one block to whoever removed the part,
+  not to its owner. The comment on line 132 says anyone may clear a block
+  whose owner has left, so any player can walk around clearing abandoned
+  blocks and collect them as stock.
+- **The abandoned-block path is broken anyway.** When no player matches,
+  `getOwnerName` falls off its end and returns no values, not `nil`.
+  `tostring(getOwnerName(part))` then errors with "missing argument #1 to
+  'tostring'", so clearing an abandoned block throws instead of working.
+- **Names instead of UserIds.** Ownership is decided by comparing names, and
+  "owner left" is detected with the string `"nil"`. A player whose username is
+  `nil` (a legal 3-letter name) would have their blocks treated as abandoned.
+
+**What to do instead:** Compare UserIds directly and drop `getOwnerName`:
+
+```lua
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+local isAbandoned = typeof(ownerId) == "number" and Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) == nil
+if not isOwner and not isAbandoned then
+	return
+end
+
+part:Destroy()
+if isOwner then
+	-- refund only the owner
+end
+```
+
+Decide whether an abandoned block refunds no one or refunds the owner's saved
+stock. Either is fine, but the person clearing it shouldn't get it.
+
+---
+
+## 5. `stock[plr]` can be `nil`, and the type hides it
+
+**Where:** `stock` declaration (line 40), reads at lines 96, 106, 139;
+`PlayerAdded` (lines 142-144).
+
+**What's wrong:** `stock[plr]` is `nil` for a player who joined before the
+`PlayerAdded` connection was made (common in Studio play-solo), and for a
+remote that is still being handled after `PlayerRemoving` ran. Every handler
+indexes the result straight away and errors. The type `{ [Player]: {...} }`
+tells the checker the lookup always succeeds, so nothing warns about this.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type the map as `{ [Player]: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]:
+number }? }` and return early when the lookup is `nil`. After connecting
+`PlayerAdded`, loop over `Players:GetPlayers()` to set up players who are
+already in the game. With finding 2's session data in place, this map becomes
+part of that per-player record.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Players can't turn receipts off
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS`
+replaces `false` with `true`, so a player who sets `ShowReceipts = false` still
+gets receipts. `AutoAnchor` at lines 118-121 gets this right.
+
+**What to do instead:** Use the same `== nil` default as `AutoAnchor`, and
+pull both into one small helper, for example
+`getBooleanSetting(player, name, default)`, so the two settings can't drift
+apart again.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Clients can flood the server output
+
+**Where:** `warn` at line 114, `print` at line 79.
+
+**What's wrong:** Every rejected placement warns and every placement prints.
+A client firing `PlaceBlock` in a loop fills the server log and hides real
+errors. More generally, none of the remotes are rate-limited.
+
+**What to do instead:** Drop the `warn`, or return the reason to the client
+through a remote for UI feedback. Remove the placement `print`, or put it
+behind a debug flag. Add a simple per-player rate limit (a timestamp or token
+bucket per remote) once the shop is in active use.
+
+---
+
+## 8. `placeBlock` takes five positional arguments, two of them boolean switches
+
+**Where:** `placeBlock` (lines 59-82), called at line 124.
+
+**What's wrong:** `placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)` doesn't say
+what `true` means at the call site. `log` is always `true`, the returned `Part`
+is never used, and the function mixes creating the part with logging.
+
+**What to do instead:** Remove `log` (see finding 7) and pass the rest as an
+options table, `placeBlock(player, center, blockType, { Anchored = autoAnchor })`,
+or keep `anchored` as the only extra argument. Type `blockType` as
+`BlockTypes.BlockType` so the `::` cast on line 66 goes away. Finding 1's
+validation produces that type.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Style and typing cleanup
+
+These are small, but they're cheap to fix now and harder later.
+
+- **No `--!strict`** (line 1). The Shared modules use it. Without it, the
+  `unknown` checks in finding 1 aren't enforced. Add it as the first line,
+  above the header comment.
+- **Abbreviated names**: `plr`, `amt`, `bt`, `p`, `pos` should be `player`,
+  `amount`, `blockType`, `otherPlayer` (or remove the loop, see finding 4),
+  `position`.
+- **`PRICES` is keyed by `string`** (line 14). Type it as
+  `{ [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }` so a block type without a price is a
+  type error. Better still, move prices into `ReplicatedStorage/Shared` (for
+  example a `Price` field on each `BlockTypes.Styles` entry) so the client shop
+  UI shows the same numbers the server charges.
+- **Functions that yield are missing the `Async` suffix**: `getCoins` /
+  `setCoins` (handled by finding 2).
+- **Anonymous handlers.** Name them for when they run (`onBuyBlocks`,
+  `onPlaceBlock`, `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`) and
+  connect them by name. This also makes them easier to test later.
+- **`getOwnerName` has no return type and returns nothing on one path**
+  (handled by finding 4, which removes it).
+- **Remotes are created at runtime** (lines 24-34). That works, but client
+  scripts need `WaitForChild` and can race on startup. Consider defining them
+  in `default.project.json` or putting them in a `Remotes` folder so they're
+  all in one place.
+
+---
